 "_doc": "c3's declaration of how the Containers page groups containers by PROJECT and by KIND (owner request 2026-11-04). Owner rule: a container belongs to the app or project it IS, not to whose data it holds. A container's project is, in order: (1) its explicit `name_overrides` entry (container name -> project id), (2) the first project whose `folder_prefix` (its cloudy-u-containers folder, containers-declared.json `dir`) or `name_prefix` matches, (3) `default` (the explicit Others bucket, no silent fallback to Cloudy). Prefixes serve containers not yet listed in name_overrides. `code` is the project's code in the fleet registry. The page strip is declared in build.json (ui.sections[containers]); a project listed there with no match here shows an empty page, honestly. `kinds` are the chips inside each project tab (All | MCPs | APIs | Apps | DBs): a container takes the first kind whose name rule (name_suffix / name_contains) matches, else the first whose category_prefix matches, else `default_kind`. `kinds[].role` matches the entity role: a git repo (data/repos_declared.json, generated from the fleet registry by data/gen_repos_slice.py) is the Repos kind. A project with `repo_group` (Cloudy) keeps the containers' own macro groups and lists its repos in that group; every other project's groups are its registry FAMILIES (CG, Capital, Hardtech, Cloud-me, Others): a repo sits in its family, a container in `container_family` (container name -> family) else in `container_group`. `group_remap` moves every container of a macro group to another group of the document (Project Apps -> Cloud Apps: projects are the tabs now), in both Topology and Table.",
